Real-world C used by tests/corpus.sh. Run tests/fetch-corpus.sh to fetch
it: the archives go in corpus/dl, are checked against the SHA-256 sums of
the ones the results were made with, and are unpacked into corpus/src.

  sqlite-amalgamation-3490100.zip   https://www.sqlite.org/2025/
  lua-5.4.7.tar.gz                  https://www.lua.org/ftp/
  zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz                 https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases
  curl-8.11.1.tar.gz                https://curl.se/download/
  musl-1.2.5.tar.gz                 https://musl.libc.org/releases/
  tcc-0.9.27.tar.bz2                https://download.savannah.gnu.org/releases/tinycc/

musl needs three generated headers, which the script makes as musl's
Makefile does:
  sed -f tools/mkalltypes.sed arch/x86_64/bits/alltypes.h.in \
      include/alltypes.h.in > obj/include/bits/alltypes.h
  cp arch/x86_64/bits/syscall.h.in obj/include/bits/syscall.h
  sed -n s/__NR_/SYS_/p < arch/x86_64/bits/syscall.h.in >> obj/include/bits/syscall.h
  echo '#define VERSION "1.2.5"' > obj/src/internal/version.h

tests/corpus.sh writes its results to corpus/results.txt (gcc) or
corpus/results-CC.txt for another reference compiler (CC=clang).
